{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Run the inventory web application on node2, listening on port 8080.","status":"satisfied","summary":"node2 had an enabled, active inventory service listening on 0.0.0.0:8080, returned a healthy node2 response, permitted TCP/8080 through firewalld, and recovered after restart with a changed boot identity.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b48b38e53fbf474d","cmd-e76898b8dae64a01","cmd-e25cfcdc19324f98","cmd-cf4d9f248b834a32","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Run the inventory web application on node3, listening on port 8080.","status":"satisfied","summary":"node3 had an enabled, active inventory service listening on 0.0.0.0:8080, returned a healthy node3 response, permitted TCP/8080 through firewalld, and recovered after restart with a changed boot identity.","evidence_ids":["cmd-08aff7bd82b540cb","cmd-db2fd195d98744b6","cmd-ae531b34894940aa","cmd-f308d4e716234fe0","global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node3:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Configure node1 to accept HTTP on port 80 and load-balance requests across node2:8080 and node3:8080.","status":"satisfied","summary":"HAProxy was active and enabled on node1, listened on port 80 with the HTTP firewall service allowed, served the inventory page, and repeated requests alternated between node2 and node3. This state recovered after node1 restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d9d955d1b4b24161","cmd-7b0359696297435c","cmd-5ed794807433414f","cmd-82d9977e0fda4dca","cmd-2625bcab9d764459","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Application files, systemd services, firewall changes, HAProxy installation/configuration, and the HAProxy SELinux connectivity setting were causally connected to the requested deployment. The three scheduled reboots served the mandatory restart-evidence protocol. Timeout probes during node3 and node1 restart windows were read-only and were followed by successful recovery checks. Final snapshots show no failed services, no package-health damage, and only task-required listeners plus normal post-reboot temporary directories.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b48b38e53fbf474d","cmd-08aff7bd82b540cb","cmd-3959e7447b904d34","cmd-d9d955d1b4b24161","cmd-b25329ae0f8f4854","cmd-0f71006c4125442a","cmd-6f0f824ae1c44e94","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node3:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All three material outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide restart protocol was performed on every managed node: each has a healthy pre-restart observation, restart acknowledgement, changed post-restart boot identity, and recovered service behavior. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate all three restarts.","limitations":["The encoded application, unit, and HAProxy configuration bodies were redacted, so their exact contents cannot be independently reviewed; final observed behavior nevertheless verifies the requested functions.","Full inventory-page content was directly asserted through node1 and identified as served by node2. Node3 was directly verified through its health endpoint and successful participation in root-path load-balanced rotation, but its full response body was not separately captured.","The restart evidence directly verifies service and cluster recovery on every node, but there is no separate per-backend persistent data-store probe; the application evidence appears to concern static inventory content."]}
